{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"Now let me think about what can be improved. The biggest costs are the 3 projections. Let me explore whether we can avoid the v-projection entirely by rearranging the computation:\n\nNote: `(p_mix @ v) * ratio = r * m @ wv^T = (r * p_mix @ x) @ wv^T = p_mix @ (r * x) @ wv^T` — no, ratio is per-token per-head so that doesn't help directly.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. `r = clamp(||u1||/||m||, 0.25, 4.0)` is scalar per (head, token). `m = (1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1 @ x`. Output is `(r * m) @ wv^T = p_mix_r @ x @ wv^T` where `p_mix_r = r * p_mix`. But computing r requires computing norms first.\n\nLet me try a different approach: compute `p_mix_r = ratio * p_mix` first, then do `p_mix_r @ v` instead of `p_mix @ v * ratio`. Same cost.\n\nActually, let me think about whether we can fuse the norm computation differently. Currently we do:\n- G = x @ x^T (B, L, L) - 21M ops for B=1,L=256\n- p1 @ G (B, HL, L) @ (B, L, L) = (B, HL, L) - 336M ops for B=1,H=20,L=256\n\nWhat if we computed norms differently? `||p@x||^2 = sum_i sum_j p_i p_j G_ij` — same as above.\n\nAlternative: compute `u = p@x` directly then `||u||`. For the largest case that's 20 * 256 * 256 * 1280 = 1.68B ops vs 336M ops for p@G. So Gram is better.\n\nWhat if we compute Gram more efficiently? Let me try:\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 -c \\\"\nimport torch, math, time\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\n\nB, L, C, H, D = 1, 256, 1280, 20, 64\nHL = H * L\nBH = B * H\n\ng = torch.Generator().manual_seed(17*1009+3)\nx = torch.randn(B, L, C, generator=g)\np1 = torch.randn(B, H, L, L, generator=g).softmax(dim=-1)\np_mix = torch.randn(B, H, L, L, generator=g).softmax(dim=-1)\n\nn = 20\n\n# Current: G + 2x bmm\ndef gram_method():\n    G = torch.bmm(x, x.transpose(1, 2))\n    p1G = torch.bmm(p1.view(B, HL, L), G).view(BH, L, L)\n    pmG = torch.bmm(p_mix.view(B, HL, L), G).view(BH, L, L)\n    norm1 = p1G.mul(p1.view(BH, L, L)).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n    normm = pmG.mul(p_mix.view(BH, L, L)).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n    return norm1, normm\n\nfor _ in range(3): gram_method()\nt0 = time.process_time_ns()\nfor _ in range(n): gram_method()\nt1 = time.process_time_ns()\nprint(f'Gram method: {(t1-t0)/n/1e6:.3f} ms')\n\n# Alternative: single fused (p1 + p_mix) bmm with concatenated p\n# We can concatenate p1 and p_mix along the middle dim and do one larger bmm\ndef fused_gram():\n    G = torch.bmm(x, x.transpose(1, 2))\n    p_both = torch.cat([p1.view(B, HL, L), p_mix.view(B, HL, L)], dim=1)  # (B, 2HL, L)\n    bothG = torch.bmm(p_both, G)  # (B, 2HL, L)\n    p1G = bothG[:, :HL, :].view(BH, L, L)\n    pmG = bothG[:, HL:, :].view(BH, L, L)\n    norm1 = p1G.mul(p1.view(BH, L, L)).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n    normm = pmG.mul(p_mix.view(BH, L, L)).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n    return norm1, normm\n\nfor _ in range(3): fused_gram()\nt0 = time.process_time_ns()\nfor _ in range(n): fused_gram()\nt1 = time.process_time_ns()\nprint(f'Fused gram:  {(t1-t0)/n/1e6:.3f} ms')\n\n# Verify equivalence\nn1a, nma = gram_method()\nn1b, nmb = fused_gram()\nprint(f'Norm1 diff: {(n1a-n1b).abs().max().item():.2e}')\nprint(f'Normm diff: {(nma-nmb).abs().max().item():.2e}')\n\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
