{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cd /workspace && python3 -c \"\nimport torch, math, time, statistics\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\n\nB, L, C, H, D = 1, 256, 1280, 20, 64\nHD = H * D\nBH = B * H\nHL = H * L\n\ng = torch.Generator().manual_seed(17*1009+3)\nx = torch.randn(B, L, C, generator=g)\nwq = torch.randn(H, D, C, generator=g) / math.sqrt(C)\nwk = torch.randn(H, D, C, generator=g) / math.sqrt(C)\nwv = torch.randn(H, D, C, generator=g) / math.sqrt(C)\nalpha, beta = 1.05, 7.5\n\n# Let me try a very different approach: compute everything in 2D using large matmuls\n# instead of 3D bmm. \n\n# Key insight: p1 is (BH, L, L), G is (B, L, L).\n# p1 @ G (per head) = p1 reshaped to (B, H*L, L) @ G -> (B, H*L, L)\n# This is a bmm with B batches.\n\n# But what about computing norm differently? \n# ||p@x||''^2 = sum_j sum_k p_ij p_ik G_jk\n# = (p * (p @ G)).sum(-1) -- that'\"'s what we do.\n\n# Alternative: Row-wise sum of squares via Cholesky? No.\n\n# Let me try: compute p0, p1, then compute p_mix, then compute ALL norms via a single\n# computation that uses both p1 and p_mix at once.\n# Actually we already tried stacking.\n\n# What about this: can we reuse the logits for something?\n# logits = q @ k\"'^T, and q = x@wq''^T/sqrt(D), k = x@wk''^T\n# So logits_ij = sum_c sum_c'\"' x_i\"'^c * (wq''^T wk / sqrt(D))_{cc'\"'} * x_j\"'^{c'\"'}\n# That's a quadratic form. G_ij = sum_c x_i\"'^c x_j''^c.\n# Not directly reusable.\n\n# Let me try a completely different strategy: can we avoid computing p0 explicitly?\n# p_mix = (1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1\n# We need: p_mix for output (p_mix @ v), and p_mix for norm.\n# We also need p1 for norm.\n# We don'\"'t need p0 directly except to compute p_mix.\n# \n# But p0 = softmax(logits) and p1 = softmax(a_p*logits + a_m*logits\"'^T).\n# We can'\"'t get p_mix without computing p0.\n\n# What if we compute softmax in log-space then exp? Already tried, slower.\n\n# Let me try one more thing