{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_72","type":"agent_message","text":"M1 (current approach) is fastest. Let me try another idea — what if we compute `pG * p` as part of the bmm somehow? \n\nNo, that's a separate elementwise multiply + reduction.\n\nLet me try something completely different. Let me check if the performance improves when we compute `G = x @ x^T` in float64 to improve accuracy and then do the p@G in float32. No, that would be slower.\n\nActually, let me try a completely fresh idea. What if we don't use the Gram matrix at all, but compute `||p@x||` more efficiently?\n\nWe know that `||p @ x||^2 = sum_d (sum_j p_ij * x_jd)^2`\n\nBut since x has shape (B, L, C), and p has shape (BH, L, L), computing p@x directly is BH * L * L * C = very expensive.\n\nBut what about this: `||p@x||^2 = rowsum( (p@x)^2 )`. That's the same as `(p@x * p@x).sum(-1)`. Still requires p@x.\n\nWhat if we factor it differently?\n`||p@x||^2 = diag(p @ G @ p^T) = (p * (p@G)).sum(-1)` — that's exactly the Gram approach.\n\nSo Gram is the most efficient way to compute these norms.\n\nLet me think about the problem from yet another angle. What if we compute everything in a different order to improve cache locality?\n\nThe current order is:\n1. q, k projections\n2. logits + softmaxes (compute p0, p1, p_mix)\n3. G + norms\n4. v projection + output\n\nWhat if we interleave:\n1. q projection\n2. k projection\n3. v projection (compute all projections together for x cache locality)\n4. logits + softmaxes\n5. G + norms\n6. output\n\nWait, we already tried fusing q+k and it didn't help much. But what about computing all three projections back-to-back? Let me check if there's any cache benefit to computing them immediately after each other (vs spreading them out).\n\nActually, this is a CPU single-threaded workload. The projections are all large GEMMs. The GEMM itself loads x from memory and writes the output. For the next GEMM, x is likely still in cache (x is only 1.3MB for the largest case). So there shouldn't be much difference.\n\nLet me try one more creative idea: can we use `torch.bmm` with `out=` to write directly into preallocated memory, saving allocation overhead?\n</parameter>\n</invoke>\n</think>Let me try to think of a more fundamental optimization. One angle I haven't fully explored: can we restructure the computation to express `p1` in a way that shares more work with `p0`?\n\nActually, let me try something different — let me benchmark each case systematically to understand the performance profile, then implement a set of targeted optimizations.\n</parameter>\n</invoke>\n</think>Let me take a step back and implement a well-polished version that incorporates multiple small improvements that together might add up, then run the full evaluation. I'll focus on:\n\n1. Minimizing allocations via in-place ops\n2. Optimal computation order for cache\n3. Efficient tensor reshaping\n4. All the proven tricks from prior rounds\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_73","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 -c \\\"\nimport torch, math, time, statistics\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\n\n# Let me try one more potentially big optimization:\n# Can we compute G AFTER we know x is 'hot' in cache from the projections?\n# Actually, let me try the reverse: compute G FIRST (small), then projections (large)\n# The idea: G is tiny (L*L), projections are big. If G is computed first and then\n# sits in cache while projections run, it might be available when we need it for p@G.\n\n# But p@G happens AFTER projections AND softmaxes. So G would need to survive in cache\n# through all of that. L2/L3 cache might still have it though.\n\n# Let me try a comprehensive optimization with the following changes:\n# 1. Compute G first (small matmul, stays in cache)\n# 2. Compute q, k projections\n# 3. Compute logits, softmax, p_mix\n# 4. Compute norms using G (G is hopefully still in L3 cache)\n# 5. Compute v projection + output\n\n# This is a different ordering than current (which does q,k,softmax,G,norms,v,output)\n\nB, L, C, H, D = 1, 256, 1280, 20, 64\nHD = H * D\nBH = B * H\nHL = H * L\n\ng = torch.Generator().manual_seed(17*1009+3)\nx = torch.randn(B, L, C, generator=g)\nwq = torch.randn(H, D, C, generator=g) / math.sqrt(C)\nwk = torch.randn(H, D, C, generator=g) / math.sqrt(C)\nwv = torch.randn(H, D, C, generator=g) / math.sqrt(C)\nalpha, beta = 1.05, 7.5\n\nimport sys\nsys.path.insert(0, '/workspace/solution')\nfrom method import attention as current\n\ndef g_first(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta):\n    B, L, C = x.shape\n    H, D = wq.shape[0], wq.shape[1]\n    inv_sqrt_d = 1.0 / math.sqrt(D)\n    HD = H * D\n    BH = B * H\n    HL = H * L\n    xr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\n\n    wqr = wq.reshape(HD, C)\n    wkr = wk.reshape(HD, C)\n    wvr = wv.reshape(HD, C)\n\n    # Compute G first (small, puts G in cache)\n    G = torch.bmm(x, x.transpose(1, 2))\n\n    # Then q, k projections\n    q = (xr @ wqr.T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n    k = (xr @ wkr.T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n    q.mul_(inv_sqrt_d)\n\n    logits = torch.bmm(q, k.transpose(1, 2))\n    del q, k\n    p0 = logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n    a_p = 0.5 + 0.5 * alpha\n    a_m = 0.5 - 0.5 * alpha\n    p1_logits = logits.mul(a_p).add_(logits.transpose(1, 2), alpha=a_m)\n    del logits\n    p1 = p1_logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n    del p1_logits\n    p_mix = torch.lerp(p0, p1, beta)\n    del p0\n\n    # Use G (should still be in cache)\n    p1G = torch.bmm(p1.view(B, HL, L), G).view(BH, L, L)\n    pmG = torch.bmm(p_mix.view(B, HL, L), G).view(BH, L, L)\n    del G\n\n    norm1 = p1G.mul_(p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n    del p1, p1G\n    normm = pmG.mul_(p_mix).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n    del pmG\n    ratio = norm1.div_(normm).clamp_(0.25, 4.0)\n    del norm1, normm\n\n    v = (xr @ wvr.T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n    y = torch.bmm(p_mix, v).mul_(ratio)\n    del p_mix, v, ratio\n    return y.view(B, H, L, D).transpose(1, 2).flatten(2).contiguous()\n\n# Check\ny0 = current(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\ny1 = g_first(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\nprint(f'Diff: {(y0-y1).abs().max().item():.2e}')\n\n# Timing\nn = 20\nt_cur = []\nt_new = []\nfor i in range(n):\n    if i % 2 == 0:\n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        current(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n        t1 = time.process_time_ns()\n        t_cur.append((t1-t0)/1e6)\n    else:\n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        g_first(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n        t1 = time.process_time_ns()\n        t_new.append((t1-t0)/1e6)\n\nprint(f'Current: {statistics.median(t_cur):.3f}ms')\nprint(f'G-first: {statistics.median(t_new):.3f}ms')\nprint(f'Speedup: {statistics.median(t_cur)/statistics.median(t_new):.3f}x')\n\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
